WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.392
Analytical datasets often contain more columns than we need for a specific task.

00:00:05.392 --> 00:00:10.102
In this example, we select one column and then two columns.

00:00:10.102 --> 00:00:16.287
When we use one set of brackets with a single column name, Pandas usually returns a Series.

00:00:16.287 --> 00:00:22.037
When we use a list of column names inside double brackets, Pandas returns a DataFrame.

00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:24.802
Click Run and compare the outputs.

00:00:24.802 --> 00:00:29.987
Selecting only the variables you need can make an analysis easier to understand.

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:35.762
Imagine a dataset with fifty columns, but your question only involves customer segment, order amount,

00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:43.407
and date. Keeping those three columns visible can reduce confusion while you develop the analysis.

00:00:43.407 --> 00:00:46.857
This also helps when sharing results with someone else.

00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:52.822
A small, focused table is often easier to review than a very wide DataFrame.

00:00:52.822 --> 00:00:58.597
Remember the key idea: one column selected directly usually gives you a Series.

00:00:58.597 --> 00:01:00.976
A list of columns gives you a DataFrame.
